      "sheet": "My Car",
      "range": "A1:C14",
      "values": [
        [
          "What I've done in my car so far",
          null,
          null
        ],
        [
          "Location",
          "Replacement",
          "Comments"
        ],
        [
          "Front door woofer",
          "Aurum Cantus AC-180F1",
          "Sounds great, but it was such a pain to mount, that I would use the 6.5\" Aurum Cantus woofer next time.  Required heavy modification of the door and grille.  There are adapter rings available now for 6.5\" woofers"
        ],
        [
          "Front door midrange",
          "Peerless by Tymphany GBS-115N25AL01-04",
          "One of the best midrange speakers I've ever heard.  12 thumbs up.  Requires a 1/8\" spacer so the dome doesn't touch the back of the grille."
        ],
        [
          "Lower A pillar tweeter",
          "Dayton Audio ND25FN-4",
          "Use 8uF capacitor for smooth integration, 9uF for slight brightness, and 10uF for very bright sound signature.  Unbelievable improvement in sound quality. Used factory housings."
        ],
        [
          "Rear door woofer",
          "Parts Express 6.5\" woofer",
          "Don't let the price scare you; this is a fantastic woofer.  Back seat sounds perfect now.  Used Metra 82-9101 with minor modification"
        ],
        [
          "Rear door woofer",
          "Dayton Audio GF180-4",
          "Better vocals and midbass clarity and less harshness than the $6 woofer.  Crossed at 4K with a 0.15mH inductor for a very smooth blend with the ND25 crossed at 5KHz."
        ],
        [
          "Rear door tweeter",
          "Dayton Audio ND25FN-4",
          "Crossed at 5Khz with 8uF capacitor.  Works well with the natural roll off of the PE woofer I used.  Makes a pretty big difference for the front seat passengers when sound focus set to \"all\""
        ],
        [
          "Subwoofer",
          "Kicker CompC10 DVC 4",
          "Frequency overlaps front door woofer. Get an amplifier with a low pass filter to at least 70hz and a bass boost no higher than 40Hz"
        ],
        [
          "Sub Amp",
          "Pioneer GM-D8701",
          "LPF to 70Hz, DVC sub wiring friendly, 1 ohm stable. Not compatible with speaker level signal from the car.  Needs a converter.  Pioneer tech support was extremel painful. Installed in the space behind the battery under the spare tire with some furniture sliding pads to fill in the gap.  The spare tire holds it down firmly."
        ],
        [
          "Sub signal converter",
          "AudioContol LC2i",
          "This isn't really the right product and the signal processing functions should be disabled but it does work and it does provide a turn on signal for your amps. Can be wired into an always on 12V source."
        ],
        [
          "Factory Amp Load",
          "47ohm 25 watt resistors bolted to a heatsink",
          "The B&O amp needs a load of no more than 50 ohms on each output or it may shut down the channel.  Many LoC's don't provide this load.  I made this one myself. NOTE: 47ohm is too high and sometimes fails the check on hot afternoons.  Suggest using 24-36 ohms instead"
        ],
        [
          "Rear deck surround",
          "FaitalPRO 4FE35",
          "Need to trim the fabric to clear the \"ears\" on the driver. Very clean mids and highs.  This speaker plays a minor role in the sound stage so it doesn't have a huge impact in sound quality, but the rear seat passengers get a big clarity boost. Keep the volume low or it sounds like an echo."
        ],
        [
          "Center Channel",
          "Peerless by Tymphany GBS-115N25AL01-04 and Dayton ND25FN-4",
          "Needed to reshape the plastic frame of the midrange extensively and also cut the pocket plastic to make room for the connections.  If you orient the connections towards front and rear, then you don't need to cut the pocket.  Installing the tweeter was pretty simple but it was smaller than the factory housing so I glued it into it's own packing foam and stuffed it in there under significant compression.  Looks terrible but works great.  Not visible under the grill anyway."
        ]
      ],
